1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome to this short talk about building reliable data pipelines.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,000
A pipeline takes raw input, validates it, transforms it, and delivers it to the systems that need it.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The first principle is to validate early. Reject bad input at the edge, before it wastes compute.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:31,000
The second principle is to normalize. Convert every input into one internal representation, so the rest of the system has a single code path.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The third principle is to make work consumable. Large jobs should be split into pieces that can be retried independently.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The fourth principle is observability. Record how long each stage takes, how much data it processed.

7
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:56,790
and why it failed. Let us talk about audio specifically. Audio arrives in many containers and codex,

8
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:07,990
such as Wave, MP3, M4A, and AUG. Sample rates vary from 8 kHz telephone audio to 48 kHz studio recordings.

9
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:16,230
Speech recognition models usually expect 16 kHz mono audio, so resampling is a required step.

10
00:01:16,230 --> 00:01:26,230
Long recordings, such as meetings or podcasts, can last for hours. Loading them entirely into memory is wasteful.

11
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:34,230
Instead, we stream the audio, cut it into chunks at natural pauses, and transcribe each chunk.

12
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:43,580
Each chunks timestamps are then shifted by the chunks offset, so the final transcript lines up with the original file.

13
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:50,580
Cutting it pauses matters, because cutting in the middle of a word produces errors at every boundary.

14
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:55,580
For production, long files should be processed asynchronously.

15
00:01:55,580 --> 00:02:00,580
The client receives a job identifier and polls for the result.

16
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:04,580
Finally, the output should be useful downstream.

17
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:12,580
Time stamps per segment allow subtitles, search, and linking an answer back to the exact moment it was said.

18
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:14,580
Thank you for listening.

19
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:19,650
